  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following statements is correct for a spontaneous polymerization reaction ?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "GRyKu",
      "text": "$\\Delta \\mathrm{G} \\lt 0, \\Delta \\mathrm{H} \\lt 0, \\Delta \\mathrm{S} \\lt 0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "2pJTM",
      "text": "$\\Delta \\mathrm{G} \\lt 0, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}\\gt0, \\Delta \\mathrm{S}\\gt0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "G4MoW",
      "text": "$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}\\gt0, \\Delta \\mathrm{H} \\lt 0, \\Delta \\mathrm{S}\\gt0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "sjIL5",
      "text": "$\\Delta G\\gt0, \\Delta \\mathrm{H}\\gt0, \\Delta \\mathrm{S}\\gt0$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hint : $n A \\rightarrow A_n$\nDuring spontaneous polymerisation reaction number of particles decreases, hence entropy decreases. Being spontaneous $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ of the reaction is less than zero.\n$\\Delta \\mathrm{G}=\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$\nTo make the $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}$ negative, $\\Delta \\mathrm{H}$ must be negative i.e. less than zero.",
